          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Field primarily symbolizes the world: the human sphere in which the word or kingdom is sown and which proceeds toward harvest and judgment. Harvest and mission are closely related applications.\",\"book_summary\":\"Field is identified as the world, sown with the word and reaped at harvest; the entry additionally associates its edge with provision for the poor and stranger and mentions hidden treasure.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Field = the world.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Field = the world.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"None material. The book adds gleaning and hidden-treasure imagery but does not alter which referent the symbol identifies.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None.\",\"rationale\":\"Both the blind consensus and the book reduce to the same explicit identification from Matthew 13:38: the field is the world. The book’s sowing and harvest language accords with the consensus’s kingdom and harvest framework. Its comments about the field’s edge and hidden treasure are supplementary associations, not a narrowing, broadening, or replacement of the core referent.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Matthew 13:38 strongly supports the core identification, and Leviticus 19:9–10 supports the statement about the field’s edge feeding the poor and stranger. However, the printed references do not directly support the hidden-treasure clause, which would ordinarily call for Matthew 13:44; this omission does not weaken support for the core identification.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
